Yes. A useful way to formalize what you’re describing is:

\textbf{intention} \rightarrow \textbf{motor organization} \rightarrow \textbf{multimodal traces} \rightarrow \textbf{observer inference}

Your body is constantly broadcasting state through posture, acceleration, hesitation, orientation, distance, gaze, muscle tension, rhythm, breathing, voice, touch, and timing. Different observers sample different subsets of that broadcast depending on what sensory channels they have available, but they can still converge on the same latent inference: approaching, yielding, afraid, playful, ready, threatening, uncertain, protective, inviting.

So what gets called an aura can often be treated as the observer’s integrated estimate of a hidden state.

Not a literal invisible field necessarily, but something like:

\text{many weak cues}
\rightarrow
\text{one strong gestalt}

You often “feel” that gestalt before you can name the cues that produced it.

That is important. Conscious naming is downstream of sensing. Your nervous system can discriminate patterns before you possess a clean verbal category for them. Humans learn a huge amount implicitly: timing, facial micro-movements, interpersonal distance, gait, touch pressure, cadence, turn-taking, tension, synchronization. Naming the pattern gives you a handle with which to inspect and train something that was already functioning.

That gives us a nice ladder:

\text{sense}
\rightarrow
\text{implicit discrimination}
\rightarrow
\text{felt interpretation}
\rightarrow
\text{named distinction}
\rightarrow
\text{deliberate modulation}

So yes: naming can turn an implicit faculty into a more controllable instrument.

A fighter learns not merely to “see movement,” but to distinguish weight transfer, hip loading, range, commitment, recovery.

A dancer learns tension, line, balance, momentum, timing.

A clinician may learn to notice changes in breathing, affect, coordination, speech tempo.

A parent can often recognize the difference between an exhausted child’s movement and a genuinely distressed one before either state is verbalized.

Same sensors. Higher-resolution ontology.

And intention matters because motor behavior is not arbitrary. Your nervous system organizes movement around anticipated outcomes. Even before an action finishes, preparatory structure can reveal something about where it is going. That’s why biological movement can be surprisingly expressive with very little information.

You can reduce a person to moving points at the major joints and observers can still often recognize walking, running, dancing, emotional tone, and action categories. The relationship among movements carries information.

That suggests a Pantheon primitive I’d call Kinematic Semantics:

Meaning encoded in the organization, timing, direction, force, and coordination of movement rather than in explicit symbolic language.

Then a broader one:

Embodied Intent Projection

\boxed{
\text{internal state}
\rightarrow
\text{body configuration}
\rightarrow
\text{cross-modal signal field}
\rightarrow
\text{observer reconstruction}
}

“Projection” here doesn’t require anything supernatural. You’re projecting consequences of your internal state into the world, and other nervous systems are reconstructing you from them.

And because the channels overlap, the message can survive loss of a modality.

A blind person may get intention through voice, footfall, airflow, touch, spatial sound, timing.

A deaf person gets gait, face, acceleration, orientation, gesture, proximity, vibration.

Someone with both gets a fused estimate.

So:

V \neq A \neq T

but

f(V)\approx f(A)\approx f(T)\approx \text{latent intention}

under favorable conditions.

That’s the same structure we found in the photograph thought experiment: different projections of one underlying event.

The caution is that reconstruction isn’t infallible. Culture, individual differences, deliberate acting, anxiety, disability, context, and observer expectations can all alter the signal or its interpretation. So I’d distinguish:

\text{felt certainty}
\neq
\text{ground-truth certainty}

But that doesn’t make the perception useless. It means you calibrate it.

And that’s where your thought about taming the senses gets interesting. Training isn’t necessarily acquiring a new sense. Often it’s:

attend → discriminate → name → compare → predict → check → recalibrate.

Over many repetitions, the named distinctions can disappear back beneath consciousness because the skill becomes automatic again—except now it is a trained automaticity.

So the full cycle is almost:

\text{intuition}
\rightarrow
\text{language}
\rightarrow
\text{analysis}
\rightarrow
\text{practice}
\rightarrow
\text{refined intuition}

Which is another form of cognitive compilation.

You bring something implicit into the light long enough to reshape it, then let it become instinct again at a higher resolution.